pend the next 15 20 minutes  talking and describing what they see  secondly like their the way it would you  know the direction of their eyes is  completely severed from the rest of the  people in the image work and then when  you're all done then you can put that  information together into some  hypotheses or hypotheses as to what you  think this is all about something  happened to him the previous night's  yeah that caused him to perhaps read  love letter that he had kept in a chest  okay and then if you can narrow it down  by looking back into the painting but  getting collaborating information do  that and then when it's all done we will  tell you what this is about as or as  much as we know what it's about so  that's why he was put in this position  because of the notoriety associated he  was a genius who wasn't recognized after  they have given a complete visual  inventory of the painting the rest of  the group has asked if anything was left  out sometimes  interestingly enough the the elephant in  the middle of the room is not addressed  and I will say to them would you like to  say something about the person in the  middle of the painting and they're going  back and forth about what this painting  is all about in what they have seen and  what we realized and realize in the  beginning but realized afterwards we've  actually taught them something about  teamwork what it means to communicate  with one  rather over some issue in the follow-up  activity where students are given  enlarged 8 by 10 color photographs of  skin lesions they are given I think 10  of them they are timed and given a  certain amount of time with each  photograph and they need to write down  specifically what it is that they see  and after we get to about number 9 or 10  the obvious things have been said and  now they are really searching for  something different and in that search  for something different they begin to  see things they wouldn't narrowly would  have passed over okay what ab